    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most latex or acrylic concrete floor paint is dry to the touch in about 1 to 2 hours, ready for a second coat in about 4 hours and ready for light foot traffic in about 24 hours at roughly 70°F and 50 percent humidity. Cars are a different story: most one-part floor paints, including the epoxy-acrylic garage floor paints, ask you to wait about 7 days before driving on them, and the film keeps hardening for two to four weeks after that. Two-part epoxy runs on its own clock, usually walkable in about a day and drivable in three to seven. Cold, damp air and a damp slab can double any of those numbers, and the label on your can is the final word because formulas vary.
        </p>

        <div id="chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does Each Type of Concrete Paint Take to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no single drying time for concrete paint because "concrete paint" covers at least four different chemistries. Water-based latex and acrylic porch and floor paint is the most common. One-part epoxy-acrylic floor paint, sold as garage floor paint or one-part epoxy, is a water-based acrylic with some epoxy resin blended in. Oil-based or alkyd porch and floor enamel still exists in some markets. And two-part epoxy kits, where you mix a resin and a hardener, cure by chemical reaction rather than by evaporation, which is why they behave so differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below gives typical label ranges at about 70°F and moderate humidity. Treat them as the normal case, not a promise. Every manufacturer publishes its own numbers on the can and on the technical data sheet, and those numbers assume the conditions printed next to them. If your basement is 58°F or your garage is humid in August, expect the longer end of each range or beyond.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Paint type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Touch dry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recoat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Light foot traffic</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vehicles</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Full cure</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Latex / acrylic porch and floor paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One-part epoxy-acrylic floor paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based / alkyd floor enamel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-8 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16-24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part water-based epoxy kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Several hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per label window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 days or more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part 100% solids epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per label window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-30 days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that the first two columns are short and similar across the water-based products, and the last two columns are long for everything. That is the pattern that catches people. A latex floor paint feels dry by lunch, so it is tempting to put the shelves back that evening and pull the car in the next morning. The surface is dry, but the film underneath is still soft and still releasing water, and anything heavy or hot sitting on it in the first week can leave a permanent mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using a two-part epoxy rather than paint, our separate guide to <a href="/garage-floor-epoxy-drying-time/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor epoxy drying time</a> goes deeper on pot life, recoat windows and the chemistry of a reacting floor. The rest of this page focuses on the paints most people buy for basements, porches and garages, which are the latex, acrylic and one-part epoxy-acrylic products we compare in our <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete floor paint buyer's guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What's the Difference Between Dry, Recoat and Cured?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Labels use four or five different milestones, and they mean different things. Dry to the touch means the surface skin has formed and will not transfer paint to a light fingertip. Nothing more. Underneath that skin, a water-based paint still contains a meaningful amount of water and coalescing solvent that has to work its way out through the film before the acrylic particles fully fuse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recoat time is the earliest point the first coat can accept a second coat without the new layer softening, lifting or wrinkling the old one. For most latex and acrylic floor paints that is around four hours in good conditions. Some one-part epoxy-acrylics and many two-part epoxies also publish a maximum recoat window. If you miss it, the first coat is too hard for the second to bite into chemically, and the label will tell you to scuff-sand before continuing. Ignoring that instruction is a common cause of coats separating later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light foot traffic means walking in socks or clean soft-soled shoes. It does not mean dragging a dehumidifier across the floor, rolling a tool chest or letting the dog run laps. Heavy traffic and furniture usually get a separate number, commonly around 48 to 72 hours for water-based floor paints. Rubber-footed furniture deserves extra caution because rubber can stick to or stain a soft film, so felt pads or a short delay make sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Full cure is the point where the film has reached close to its final hardness, abrasion resistance and chemical resistance. For acrylic and one-part floor paints, manufacturers commonly describe this as two to four weeks, and some say up to 30 days. During that window the floor is usable but more vulnerable. Avoid washing it with anything stronger than plain water, avoid area rugs that trap moisture against it, and do not judge the durability of the paint until the month is up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Touch dry is about the surface. Cure is about the whole film.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every early failure on a painted floor, from tire marks to rug imprints to scuffed paths, comes from treating the touch-dry time as if it were the cure time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="vehicles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Long Before You Can Drive on Painted Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For latex, acrylic and one-part epoxy-acrylic garage floor paints, the common label guidance is about seven days before parking a car, and that is the number to plan around. Some products say 72 hours in ideal conditions, but seven days is the safer default and it is what most one-part garage floor paints print on the can. Read your specific label, and if your garage is below about 60°F, add days rather than subtracting them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is hot-tire pickup. After a drive, tires are warm and the tread is pressing on the floor with real force. Car tires contain plasticizers and other chemicals that can soften coatings, and as the tire cools it contracts and grips the surface. On a paint that is not fully hardened, the tire can lift the film right off the concrete when you pull out the next morning, leaving bare patches in the exact shape of your tread. Our guide to <a href="/hot-tire-pickup-epoxy-garage-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hot tire pickup</a> explains the mechanism in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even after the seven days, one-part floor paints remain more vulnerable to hot-tire pickup than true two-part epoxy or polyaspartic coatings, and that weakness never fully goes away. If you park daily and drive in on hot summer tires, the cure time is only part of the answer. Thorough prep and a coating rated for vehicles matter more. Our <a href="/garage-floor-paint-vs-epoxy/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor paint vs epoxy</a> comparison covers when paint is enough and when it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the first few weeks after you start parking, a few cheap habits help. Let the car cool for a while before pulling in after a long highway drive. Avoid turning the steering wheel while the car is stationary, which grinds the tire into the film. And slide a thin rubber or plastic parking mat under each tire if you want insurance during that first month while the paint finishes curing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Do Temperature and Humidity Change Drying Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Temperature is the biggest variable. Most water-based floor paints require air and surface temperatures of at least 50°F during application and for the first day or two afterward, and many specify a range of roughly 50°F to 90°F. Below the minimum, latex and acrylic particles may not fuse into a continuous film, a problem called poor coalescence. The paint can look fine and then crack, powder or wear through quickly because it never formed properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            For two-part epoxy, a widely used rule of thumb is that cure speed roughly halves for every 18°F drop in temperature. An epoxy that is walkable in 24 hours at 70°F might need about 48 hours at 52°F. Water-based paints do not follow that rule exactly, but the direction is the same: a cold garage in November can easily double the label times, and a concrete slab often stays several degrees colder than the air above it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity mostly affects water-based products, because water has to evaporate out of the film. In humid air, evaporation slows and both the touch-dry and recoat times stretch. A basement at 75 percent relative humidity can leave a latex floor paint tacky for hours longer than the can suggests. Humid conditions also raise the risk of condensation on a cool slab, which is one reason labels tell you to keep the surface at least 5°F above the dew point during application.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat is not automatically good either. In direct summer sun or above about 90°F, the top of the film can skin over before the paint underneath has released its water, which leads to bubbles, lap marks and a weak layer. Outdoors, paint patios and porches in the morning after the dew has burned off or in the late afternoon once the slab has cooled, and check the forecast for no rain for at least 24 hours.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Label Conditions</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50-90°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common application range for water-based floor paint</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum margin above dew point</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rain-free window often required outdoors</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Longer cure at about 18°F colder (epoxy rule of thumb)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="slab" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does a Damp Slab Make Paint Take Longer to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and it can do worse than slow it down. Concrete is porous, and slabs on grade, especially basement floors and slabs without a vapor barrier underneath, can pass a steady stream of moisture vapor up from the soil. Water-based paint on a damp slab dries slowly because the moisture keeps arriving from below while it tries to evaporate from above. Worse, moisture pressure under the film is a leading cause of blistering and peeling months later.
          </p>

          <p>
            New concrete needs time before you paint it at all. Most floor paint labels say to wait at least 28 to 30 days after a new pour so the slab can cure and give off much of its excess water. That figure is about the concrete, not the paint, and our guide on <a href="/how-long-does-concrete-take-to-cure/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long concrete takes to cure</a> explains why the slab keeps changing for weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            On an older slab, do a simple moisture check before you start. Tape a square of clear plastic sheeting, about 18 by 18 inches, tightly to the floor on all four sides and leave it at least 16 to 24 hours. If droplets form under the plastic or the concrete underneath darkens, the slab is passing enough moisture to cause trouble. The method and its limits are covered in our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete moisture test guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the slab is wet, the drying time on the can will not apply to you, and no amount of waiting after painting fixes it. The answer is to deal with the water source first, or to choose a breathable penetrating product rather than a film. Persistent dampness or seepage in a basement floor is a separate problem worth reading about in our <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water coming through the basement floor</a> guide before you buy any paint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ready" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Can You Tell When Concrete Paint Is Ready?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The label times are the starting point, but you can check the floor itself. For recoating, press a fingertip lightly in an out-of-the-way corner. If it feels cool or slightly tacky, or leaves a print, wait. Cool usually means water is still evaporating. A properly dry film feels room temperature and smooth, and a fingertip slides over it without drag.
          </p>

          <p>
            For heavier use, try the thumbnail test in an inconspicuous spot after the foot-traffic time has passed. Press a thumbnail firmly into the film. If it leaves a lasting dent, the paint is still soft and should not get furniture, equipment or tires. If the nail barely marks it, the film is firming up. This is a rough check, not a measurement, but it reliably catches a floor that is behind schedule because of cold or damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smell is another clue. Water-based floor paints give off a mild odor while they are still releasing water and coalescing solvents. A persistent smell after several days in a closed basement usually means slow drying and poor ventilation. Oil-based enamels smell stronger and longer, and they also yellow more in low light, which is one reason they have largely given way to water-based products for interior floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep track of real conditions. A cheap thermometer and hygrometer on the floor tell you whether you were inside the label's assumptions. If the garage sat at 55°F and 70 percent humidity all week, the seven-day vehicle number on the can was written for a different room. Add time until the thumbnail test passes, then add another day for safety before parking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Can You Make Concrete Paint Dry Faster Without Ruining It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most effective step happens before you open the can: apply thin coats. Two thin coats dry and cure far faster than one heavy coat, and they bond better. A thick coat traps water and solvent under its own skin, stays soft for days, and is more prone to peeling. Most floor paint labels give a spread rate, often in the range of 300 to 400 square feet per gallon on smooth concrete, and staying close to that is the easiest way to hit the label drying times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation and dehumidification come next. Moving air carries moisture away from the surface. In a garage, open the door partway once the paint is touch dry and the air outside is not damp. In a basement, run a dehumidifier and a fan pointed across the room rather than directly at the wet paint, which can blow dust onto it and skin the surface too quickly. Keep humidity in the 40 to 60 percent range if you can.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need heat, warm the space evenly and safely. An electric space heater placed well away from the floor and anything flammable can bring a cold basement up to the label minimum. Avoid unvented propane or kerosene heaters in enclosed spaces. They add water vapor and combustion gases, can create a carbon monoxide hazard, and are an ignition risk around solvent-bearing products. Heat the room for a day before painting so the slab warms too, not just the air.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does not work is rushing the schedule once the paint is down. A floor that is touch dry in two hours is not ready for traffic in three because you aimed a fan at it. If a deadline matters, plan the job around it: paint on a warm, dry weekend, give foot traffic its full day, and leave the car out for the week. If you cannot spare a week, a fast-curing polyaspartic coating is the faster product, not a faster version of paint. For general application technique, see our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-concrete-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to paint a concrete floor</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking the car after two days</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most one-part garage floor paints ask for about seven days before vehicle traffic because the film is still soft. Warm tires on a half-cured film are the classic cause of hot-tire pickup, where the paint lifts away in the shape of the tread. Waiting the full week costs nothing and saves you from starting over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a cold slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Air temperature on the weather app is not slab temperature. A garage slab in autumn can sit well below 50°F even on a mild afternoon. Below the label minimum, water-based paint may never coalesce into a continuous film, so it dries slowly and wears through fast. Check the floor with a thermometer before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying one heavy coat to save time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single thick coat feels efficient, but it traps water under its own skin and can stay soft for days. It also cures unevenly and is more likely to blister or peel. Two thin coats at the label spread rate dry faster overall and leave a tougher, better bonded film than one heavy pass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating outside the label window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some one-part epoxy-acrylics and most two-part epoxies publish a maximum recoat time. Miss it and the second coat cannot bond chemically to the first, which can lead to sheets of topcoat peeling off later. If you go past the window, scuff-sand the first coat lightly, vacuum, and then apply the second coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting rugs and furniture back too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rugs trap moisture against a film that is still releasing it, and rubber feet can stick to or discolor soft paint. Give furniture at least the heavy-traffic time on the label, often two to three days, and keep rugs off for a couple of weeks while the paint finishes curing toward full hardness.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I walk on concrete paint after 4 hours?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually only lightly, and only in socks, if at all. Four hours is the typical recoat time for water-based floor paint, which means the film can accept another coat, not traffic. Most labels ask for about 24 hours before light foot traffic. If you must cross the floor, lay down a clean board or cardboard path and avoid twisting your feet on the paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait between coats of concrete floor paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most latex and acrylic porch and floor paints, about four hours at roughly 70°F and moderate humidity. One-part epoxy-acrylics range from about four hours to a day, and oil-based enamels usually need 16 to 24 hours. Cold or humid conditions stretch those times. If the first coat still feels cool or tacky, wait longer before rolling the second.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete paint dry faster outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, if the weather cooperates. A warm, breezy, low-humidity day dries water-based paint quickly. But direct sun on a hot slab can skin the surface too fast and cause bubbles or lap marks, and evening dew can land on a film that is not yet dry. Paint outdoor slabs mid-morning or late afternoon with no rain forecast for a full day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does painted concrete take to fully cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most water-based and one-part epoxy-acrylic floor paints reach close to full hardness in about two to four weeks, and some manufacturers say up to 30 days. Oil-based enamels take a similar or longer time. During that period the floor is usable but softer, so avoid harsh cleaners, heavy rolling loads, rugs and dragging furniture until the month is up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my concrete paint still tacky after a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual causes are cold, humidity, a coat that went on too thick, or moisture coming up through the slab. Raise the room temperature with a safe electric heater, run a dehumidifier and improve air movement. If it is still tacky after several days in good conditions, suspect slab moisture or an incompatible coating underneath, and test before adding more paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after painting a basement floor can I put furniture back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on at least 48 to 72 hours for most water-based floor paints, longer if the basement is cool or damp. Use felt pads rather than bare rubber feet, lift pieces into place rather than dragging them, and keep area rugs off for about two weeks so trapped moisture does not soften or discolor the paint while it cures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete stain take to dry compared with paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based penetrating concrete stains often dry to the touch in about an hour and can take foot traffic within roughly a day, similar to paint. Acid stains need to react, typically for several hours, then be neutralized, rinsed and dried before sealing. The sealer applied on top then sets the real schedule, which is often a day for foot traffic.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

