        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan on about 1 to 2 hours to touch dry, about 4 hours between coats, about 24 hours before walking on it and about a week before driving on most concrete floor paints, with full hardness arriving over the following two to four weeks. Those numbers assume a warm, dry room and a dry slab. Cold, humidity, thick coats and moisture from below all push them longer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The paint can only dry as fast as the conditions allow, so set up the conditions first: test the slab, warm the space, thin coats, steady airflow. Then give the floor its full schedule. A week of parking in the driveway is a small price compared with stripping a peeled garage floor and starting again.
          </p>
        </div>

